1
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:13,392
[SPEAKER_01]: I was just thinking about that, eh, ordination and certificates from institutions, you know, regarding ordination, basically.

2
00:00:18,533 --> 00:00:19,433
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something you need.

3
00:00:21,293 --> 00:00:23,213
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something to practice.

4
00:00:23,714 --> 00:00:27,434
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need divine rights to practice.

5
00:00:28,054 --> 00:00:31,435
[SPEAKER_01]: You need authority to give you those rights, those certificates.

6
00:00:34,863 --> 00:00:38,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it wasn't like that in the beginning.

7
00:00:56,507 --> 00:01:01,370
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, mind you, the ordination and the whole thing we go through, that did come from the Buddha, it evolved in that way.

8
00:01:02,311 --> 00:01:04,112
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a valid thing.

9
00:01:04,152 --> 00:01:04,893
[SPEAKER_00]: But I know what you mean.

10
00:01:05,413 --> 00:01:16,981
[SPEAKER_00]: All of the other kind of politics involved with the saṅgha in the West, in Thailand, in Sri Lanka, all of the all of the polities that now exist and so on, that all came later.

11
00:01:21,024 --> 00:01:24,667
[SPEAKER_00]: And, and the, and anyway, there's nothing to do with it.

12
00:01:25,236 --> 00:01:34,204
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yes, in the early days, remember when the Buddha was just walking around and had all of it, he would be, oh, wow, that dhamma seems really true, can I follow you?

13
00:01:34,684 --> 00:01:37,287
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, yes, come here, bhikkhu.

14
00:01:37,347 --> 00:01:40,770
[SPEAKER_00]: He would address him bhikkhu, and that would mean he's now part of the order.

15
00:01:41,270 --> 00:01:42,091
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all it took.

16
00:01:42,952 --> 00:01:46,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Because back then, well, it was all about the dhamma, so...

17
00:01:47,658 --> 00:01:50,699
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the bond, so to speak.

18
00:01:50,799 --> 00:02:01,064
[SPEAKER_00]: It was clear what the dhamma was, it was clear what the practice was, so people knew it straight and there would be enough for them to join and make the effort and become arahants.

19
00:02:01,744 --> 00:02:06,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, obviously, in a course of time, over the years, things were getting a bit more complicated.

20
00:02:06,707 --> 00:02:11,629
[SPEAKER_00]: The rules were being made because the monks were breaking, doing unwholesome stuff and so on.

21
00:02:12,445 --> 00:02:23,768
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it evolved to the point, okay, now you need a senior monk, you need at least ten other bhikkhus present, and then you can have upasampada, ordination, and so on and so forth.

22
00:02:24,248 --> 00:02:27,269
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's all, again, that's all still relatively simple.

23
00:02:27,429 --> 00:02:29,990
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's also... and that's an issue as well, actually.

24
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:36,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think, right now, now, I'm sort of safe, I am practicing now... Well, that's the thing.

25
00:02:36,712 --> 00:02:39,673
[SPEAKER_00]: I was going to say that the reason why it actually evolved

26
00:02:41,082 --> 00:02:42,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, proliferate.

27
00:02:42,363 --> 00:02:44,084
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a form of involvement.

28
00:02:44,165 --> 00:02:45,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Proliferate more than that.

29
00:02:46,106 --> 00:02:46,847
[SPEAKER_00]: It's exactly that.

30
00:02:46,887 --> 00:02:50,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now, people don't have the dhamma.

31
00:02:51,535 --> 00:02:54,877
[SPEAKER_00]: They might find it on the individual level, through their individual efforts.

32
00:02:55,377 --> 00:03:05,403
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, when you come to any order, be Theravāda, Zen, whatever, on an average level, what you hear being taught, it's nothing to... it's very little... well, it's not dhamma, basically.

33
00:03:05,423 --> 00:03:10,426
[SPEAKER_00]: You might be talking about the dhamma, but listening to it, you won't become an arahant.

34
00:03:10,946 --> 00:03:13,988
[SPEAKER_00]: You... even if you make the effort upon it, it's basically wrong.

35
00:03:15,301 --> 00:03:19,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because of that, means there is no that kind of clear seeing of the dhamma.

36
00:03:19,585 --> 00:03:25,110
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha meant when he said the dhamma will die 500 years after his passing away.

37
00:03:25,290 --> 00:03:27,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean it will be impossible to become an arahant later.

38
00:03:27,732 --> 00:03:35,880
[SPEAKER_00]: It means that when it's being spoken, when it's being referred to, when it's being quoted, it's most likely going to be wrong.

39
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:39,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, it's going to refer to the wrong thing as the dhamma.

40
00:03:40,204 --> 00:03:41,164
[SPEAKER_00]: But it won't be the dhamma.

41
00:03:42,593 --> 00:03:51,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, as I said, a person can dig through that and find it on an individual level, but not on the level of community, society, religion, teachers, whatever else.

42
00:03:51,903 --> 00:03:55,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for that very reason, people, exactly, they need this form of belonging.

43
00:03:56,388 --> 00:04:00,652
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they need something more significant to make them feel...

44
00:04:03,662 --> 00:04:09,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, to feel a certain bond with whatever they want to do with this life, so with the holy life, with the order they're joining.

45
00:04:09,987 --> 00:04:16,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you... if somebody just comes off the street and you say, okay, come here, Bhikkhu, here is the door, he most likely won't take it serious.

46
00:04:17,145 --> 00:04:18,026
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter how much he wants.

47
00:04:18,106 --> 00:04:18,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

48
00:04:18,406 --> 00:04:23,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, well, his personal attitude, his personal reasons for practice are not clear to himself.

49
00:04:24,271 --> 00:04:31,637
[SPEAKER_00]: If, on the other hand, a fully self-transparent, authentic person, puthujjana, comes off the street, there will be enough of him.

50
00:04:31,977 --> 00:04:37,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he knows the place of it, he knows what he's supposed to do, he knows why he is here for.

51
00:04:39,061 --> 00:04:47,290
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he won't ever need that external, artificial, superficial sense of, I belong to something bigger, or these robes, or whatever else.

52
00:04:47,651 --> 00:04:58,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes can help to people in the beginning, but it's not really essential, because if they don't start clearing up the bad faith internally, they'll never

53
00:05:00,633 --> 00:05:09,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they never really made the proper effort, which means they never really discovered dharma, which means they will never ever be the valid, the right type of connection with it.

54
00:05:09,877 --> 00:05:18,621
[SPEAKER_00]: It will all be maintained by the external circumstances, such as the robes, the rules, the community you live in, they will keep you together.

55
00:05:18,921 --> 00:05:22,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the practice of the wisdom that you develop, because Arahant, he doesn't have to.

56
00:05:26,448 --> 00:05:34,475
[SPEAKER_00]: But he will, because that's the lifestyle that corresponds the best to Arahantship, which is why he would have created it in such ways.

57
00:05:42,621 --> 00:05:50,568
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a... it's a... if you were to actually only pick up, like, for example, monks who ordained

58
00:05:53,041 --> 00:05:56,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Clear reasons, without bad faith, relatively authentic.

59
00:05:57,525 --> 00:05:58,465
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't find many.

60
00:06:02,889 --> 00:06:12,156
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't find many, which means that most of the people rely on the external form to keep them together.

61
00:06:12,577 --> 00:06:14,598
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is a form of sīlabata paramāsa.

62
00:06:15,439 --> 00:06:17,220
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see the place of the rules.

63
00:06:17,921 --> 00:06:20,363
[SPEAKER_00]: You think the virtue is the practice.

64
00:06:21,581 --> 00:06:24,622
[SPEAKER_00]: You think keeping your rules together is the dhamma.

65
00:06:24,962 --> 00:06:26,802
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the practice is, but it isn't.

66
00:06:28,422 --> 00:06:29,323
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a prerequisite.

67
00:06:30,003 --> 00:06:34,604
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something that needs to be in place, but it's not the practice.

68
00:06:37,304 --> 00:06:45,526
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I said to you probably in the past as well, so you have to find out how can you not keep the rules without breaking them.

69
00:06:46,502 --> 00:06:49,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Because breaking the rules, again, that's against the practice, for sure.

70
00:06:50,563 --> 00:06:52,744
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, holding the rules is also against the practice.

71
00:06:52,904 --> 00:06:57,945
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you not break the rules, but then not be... not... not keep them either?

72
00:07:00,346 --> 00:07:03,947
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you let go of syllabus, but must let go, or rather put it in the right place.

73
00:07:09,009 --> 00:07:10,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you guys... you guys, eh...

74
00:07:11,611 --> 00:07:17,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Eight precepts, the basic, the fundamentals of Ayurveda, you know, basic precepts.

75
00:07:17,914 --> 00:07:28,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else, you know, celibacy, part of it, but everything else is something very necessary, old.

76
00:07:29,258 --> 00:07:29,858
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

77
00:07:29,958 --> 00:07:32,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Eight precepts are the kind of core of it.

78
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:34,580
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I would always refer to.

79
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:39,402
[SPEAKER_00]: When you would be talking about the olden times, the wise men of the past, it would always be around the eight precepts.

80
00:07:39,442 --> 00:07:40,963
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be the symbol of virtue.

81
00:07:41,592 --> 00:07:46,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And morality established, that can lead to wisdom, if a person makes the right effort.

82
00:07:47,135 --> 00:07:49,456
[SPEAKER_01]: And the rest is just community growing?

83
00:07:49,997 --> 00:07:55,620
[SPEAKER_00]: The rest of it, as he said himself, on his deathbed, it was utterly circumstantial.

84
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:03,184
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, you can do away with most of the rules, smaller rules, that I've made.

85
00:08:03,364 --> 00:08:03,844
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not.

86
00:08:04,404 --> 00:08:06,766
[SPEAKER_00]: They were just a response to a recent problem.

87
00:08:09,075 --> 00:08:22,247
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, Ananda forgot to ask what those rules were, so then the Arhat said, well, let's just keep them, because people will, and they would have criticized right away, saying, ah, look, the teacher died, and now they abolished half of the rules.

88
00:08:22,287 --> 00:08:22,727
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

89
00:08:22,828 --> 00:08:23,628
[SPEAKER_00]: The order is done.

90
00:08:25,170 --> 00:08:26,211
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a, you know...

91
00:08:26,351 --> 00:08:32,096
[SPEAKER_01]: But maybe the way you said it now, maybe you said it in a similar way, like, oh, you could actually

92
00:08:33,772 --> 00:08:36,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, it could have been like that.

93
00:08:36,954 --> 00:08:38,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, yes, I see what you mean.

94
00:08:39,036 --> 00:08:43,419
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's not because there were... the rules were kind of in different

95
00:08:57,744 --> 00:09:00,807
[SPEAKER_00]: They make no difference to the practice of the dhamma, whether you practice or not.

96
00:09:00,827 --> 00:09:02,068
[SPEAKER_00]: It just makes no difference to it.

97
00:09:02,668 --> 00:09:07,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, having them or not, most of those smaller rules we talked about, having them or not, doesn't make a difference.

98
00:09:08,973 --> 00:09:25,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, making a big point out of a portion and really finding out, it's... well, it's a waste of time and most likely, it might be coming out of something unwholesome, if somebody's so keen to get rid of them.

99
00:09:25,847 --> 00:09:26,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know,

100
00:09:26,999 --> 00:09:27,659
[SPEAKER_00]: revolution.

101
00:09:27,739 --> 00:09:29,960
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm gonna make a new order or something.

102
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:30,420
[SPEAKER_00]: It's silly.

103
00:09:30,820 --> 00:09:31,701
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's a waste of time.

104
00:09:31,721 --> 00:09:33,021
[SPEAKER_00]: It's worrying.

105
00:09:33,401 --> 00:09:35,042
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, in the beginning, there were no rules.

106
00:09:36,482 --> 00:09:37,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there was one rule.

107
00:09:37,642 --> 00:09:38,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there were two rules.

108
00:09:38,843 --> 00:09:39,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there were three rules.

109
00:09:39,963 --> 00:09:40,783
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how it evolved.

110
00:09:41,143 --> 00:09:44,565
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is, you know, the famous story of that monk who came and said, oh, I just...

111
00:09:44,705 --> 00:09:45,525
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to disrobe.

112
00:09:46,916 --> 00:09:54,879
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not, she said, I'm not, I'm not unhappy with the dhamma or anything like that, but there are way too many rules and I cannot possibly keep...

113
00:09:55,019 --> 00:10:01,841
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, I can't keep all 147 rules, which was funny, because now we have 227.

114
00:10:02,721 --> 00:10:06,682
[SPEAKER_00]: And, which means, when this monk was complaining, not all of them were yet created.

115
00:10:07,643 --> 00:10:08,783
[SPEAKER_00]: But he said, it's just too much.

116
00:10:08,983 --> 00:10:10,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I can't possibly keep that in my mind.

117
00:10:10,864 --> 00:10:11,964
[SPEAKER_00]: I won't, you know, I don't want to do it.

118
00:10:12,004 --> 00:10:15,105
[SPEAKER_00]: And everybody says, okay, well, can you keep three rules then?

119
00:10:17,912 --> 00:10:20,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't do anything unwholesome by body, by speech, or mind.

120
00:10:20,795 --> 00:10:22,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, I think, there might be another story.

121
00:10:22,257 --> 00:10:28,383
[SPEAKER_00]: In this case, he just said... he said... Can you just... No, I think that came from Ajahn Chah.

122
00:10:28,423 --> 00:10:29,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Just watch your mind, or something like that.

123
00:10:30,385 --> 00:10:33,749
[SPEAKER_00]: No, this was like, don't do anything unwholesome by body, by speech, or by mind.

124
00:10:33,949 --> 00:10:34,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you do that?

125
00:10:34,650 --> 00:10:36,271
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, yeah, of course, that's easy.

126
00:10:37,153 --> 00:10:41,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, because he knew what Dharma also was, but he couldn't be bothered about the specifics.

127
00:10:42,216 --> 00:10:43,917
[SPEAKER_00]: This is how you wear your robe.

128
00:10:44,117 --> 00:10:46,159
[SPEAKER_00]: This is how you put your sitting cloth.

129
00:10:46,179 --> 00:10:47,159
[SPEAKER_00]: You're breaking the rule.

130
00:10:47,700 --> 00:10:49,581
[SPEAKER_00]: It is ridiculous, in a way.

131
00:10:50,682 --> 00:10:53,303
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that man had a point, and the Buddha knew that.

132
00:10:53,323 --> 00:10:54,244
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he said, well, okay.

133
00:10:55,124 --> 00:10:55,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Fair enough.

134
00:10:56,445 --> 00:10:57,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people can see it like that.

135
00:10:57,686 --> 00:11:00,328
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can you keep the three rules?

136
00:11:01,220 --> 00:11:01,700
[SPEAKER_00]: And do that.

137
00:11:02,260 --> 00:11:08,203
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what refers to, like in the suttas, abhidhamma and abhivinna.

138
00:11:08,263 --> 00:11:09,083
[SPEAKER_00]: It refers to that.

139
00:11:09,183 --> 00:11:12,664
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just more, well, more detailed, higher, in a sense.

140
00:11:13,144 --> 00:11:15,065
[SPEAKER_00]: You surmount it, you come to the core.

141
00:11:15,865 --> 00:11:18,906
[SPEAKER_00]: The point of every rule is not to do anything unwholesome.

142
00:11:19,707 --> 00:11:24,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, if you're focused on not doing anything unwholesome, automatically, you cannot break the rule.

143
00:11:25,089 --> 00:11:28,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if, actually, externally, you're putting your sitting cloth wrong.

144
00:11:30,349 --> 00:11:31,930
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not breaking the rule in that way.

145
00:11:32,330 --> 00:11:42,998
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's breaking the circumstantial irrelevant rule, for which you might be told off, and for which you won't feel anything, because, you know, it doesn't pertain to anything of significance.

146
00:11:43,478 --> 00:11:46,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why you won't have a problem keeping it either.

147
00:11:48,541 --> 00:11:50,483
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't make a difference.

148
00:11:51,003 --> 00:11:59,569
[SPEAKER_00]: If some of these people get too wound up, you might not want to... you might want to make a point to them, that it's their mistake, but that's more related to ridiculous koans and stuff that comes.

149
00:12:00,442 --> 00:12:01,362
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes with the communities.

150
00:12:01,442 --> 00:12:03,883
[SPEAKER_00]: But when it comes to rules, it works like that.

151
00:12:03,903 --> 00:12:06,884
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know what's important and you know there is no compromise there.

152
00:12:07,504 --> 00:12:22,070
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, the other rules are just kind of involved in a form of a response to the recent situations for the duration of the Buddha's lifetime, while he was around.

153
00:12:26,271 --> 00:12:28,872
[SPEAKER_00]: But, technically and theoretically,

154
00:12:29,892 --> 00:12:32,355
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to keep the eight precepts strictly.

155
00:12:32,696 --> 00:12:38,423
[SPEAKER_00]: The stricter you keep them, the... well, the sharper you are, the more precise you can get.

156
00:12:40,806 --> 00:12:41,306
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

157
00:12:43,008 --> 00:12:47,955
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, you know, if sometimes people are like, oh, too much, like, oh, there's this little rule and that little rule and...

158
00:12:49,367 --> 00:13:01,676
[SPEAKER_00]: I must not do this, and I can't move the soil, but I can move the soil if it's been disturbed, or it's been at least four weeks before, or three months, or too much... well, the rain has the... it's just... it is too complicated, and it's utterly non-essential.

159
00:13:02,917 --> 00:13:18,709
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if on the other hand, we focus on the eight precepts, and you really keep an eye on them, so not even by mind that you're engaging in breaking them, in terms of the unwholesome thought, then even if you go and you take the ground,

160
00:13:19,291 --> 00:13:19,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

161
00:13:19,671 --> 00:13:25,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Externally, it's an offense, and you should confess it, and somebody will tell you off.

162
00:13:25,537 --> 00:13:29,862
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you know you're breaking that rule, so to speak, you won't do it.

163
00:13:30,402 --> 00:13:31,303
[SPEAKER_00]: You know it's irrelevant.

164
00:13:31,884 --> 00:13:40,212
[SPEAKER_00]: But you know that if you don't do it, there is even no... there's zero ground to be harassed, to be hassled by somebody telling you off, or, like, shouldn't be doing that.

165
00:13:40,452 --> 00:13:42,354
[SPEAKER_00]: It's utterly irrelevant whether you do it or not.

166
00:13:42,414 --> 00:13:43,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes no difference.

167
00:13:44,445 --> 00:13:47,047
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is that element of, oh, well, other people don't know that.

168
00:13:47,527 --> 00:13:49,529
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they will most likely hassle me.

169
00:13:50,049 --> 00:13:50,750
[SPEAKER_00]: And I don't...

170
00:13:50,770 --> 00:13:51,431
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to be hassled.

171
00:13:51,451 --> 00:13:52,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would I be hassled?

172
00:13:52,311 --> 00:13:53,252
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a distraction.

173
00:13:53,672 --> 00:13:55,534
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to deal with other people's issues.

174
00:13:56,415 --> 00:13:57,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you just don't do it.

175
00:13:57,816 --> 00:13:58,857
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it seems like, yeah.

176
00:14:00,017 --> 00:14:02,980
[SPEAKER_01]: But we don't get hassled, yeah, or whatever.

177
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,723
[SPEAKER_01]: But, seriously, I think a lot of people choose just to be self-aware.

178
00:14:07,543 --> 00:14:09,005
[SPEAKER_00]: It just... Yeah.

179
00:14:09,165 --> 00:14:09,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

180
00:14:09,665 --> 00:14:09,925
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

181
00:14:10,105 --> 00:14:10,566
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

182
00:14:11,046 --> 00:14:12,167
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't actually... Yeah.

183
00:14:12,427 --> 00:14:13,208
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't really...

184
00:14:15,350 --> 00:14:18,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's their reasons, maybe, to be...

185
00:14:18,471 --> 00:14:19,672
[SPEAKER_00]: It often actually is, yeah.

186
00:14:19,712 --> 00:14:27,574
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the few people I've met that have been monks, and there were samaneras, and then monks, and then samaneras, it was usually revolving around that.

187
00:14:27,694 --> 00:14:33,936
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just, they felt it was too restrictive, for no significant reasons.

188
00:14:34,536 --> 00:14:40,837
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can see their point, because it's not just that it's too restrictive from the vinaya point of view, but it's too restrictive from the cultural point of view.

189
00:14:41,378 --> 00:14:42,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Kovat point of view.

190
00:14:43,356 --> 00:14:50,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, in Sri Lanka, they might not have so much emphasis on kovar as they do in the West or Thailand, but certainly, it's there.

191
00:14:50,862 --> 00:14:52,424
[SPEAKER_00]: There are things, oh, no, that's not proper.

192
00:14:53,364 --> 00:15:02,772
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all... it's... it's often relatively random, depending on what... what monk tell... what monk tells you there is some proper... that's improper or not, but it's still there.

193
00:15:03,353 --> 00:15:06,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you shouldn't be holding the broom like that, or... What?

194
00:15:07,575 --> 00:15:08,295
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's there.

195
00:15:08,575 --> 00:15:11,636
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all of that can actually wind people up.

196
00:15:12,376 --> 00:15:20,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... if somebody's serious with their practice, then, yeah, it doesn't make a difference whether you're a samanera or a bhikkhu.

197
00:15:20,798 --> 00:15:22,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it doesn't make a difference even if you're a layman.

198
00:15:23,239 --> 00:15:28,640
[SPEAKER_00]: By the way, it's much harder as a layman because wearing the robe is also a form of statement for others.

199
00:15:28,680 --> 00:15:30,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, it implies a lot.

200
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:32,761
[SPEAKER_00]: It tells others what you are, so you don't have to explain.

201
00:15:33,701 --> 00:15:37,482
[SPEAKER_00]: If you just turn up as a layman in a group of laypeople, they'll think you're a layman.

202
00:15:38,643 --> 00:15:41,784
[SPEAKER_00]: You might be anāgāmī in your mind, but they think you're a layman.

203
00:15:41,804 --> 00:15:48,627
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they'll treat you like that, they'll expose you to lay things, they'll hassle you with lay things, and so on.

204
00:15:49,908 --> 00:15:50,828
[SPEAKER_00]: As a monk, they won't.

205
00:15:51,188 --> 00:15:54,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if they do, they will at least know that you're not a layman.

206
00:15:55,170 --> 00:15:56,371
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, they might choose to ignore that.

207
00:15:56,411 --> 00:15:59,252
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's a different thing.

208
00:15:59,272 --> 00:16:00,352
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is a formal statement.

209
00:16:01,313 --> 00:16:02,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, by wearing the robe.

210
00:16:04,354 --> 00:16:05,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Which was also the time in India.

211
00:16:06,767 --> 00:16:09,188
[SPEAKER_00]: They could have... and then, yeah, some of them just went off.

212
00:16:09,228 --> 00:16:12,750
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, they would put on any robes and they would immediately stand up.

213
00:16:12,890 --> 00:16:17,592
[SPEAKER_00]: He's a sādhu, he's a religious person, he's the seeker of the spiritual life.

214
00:16:18,332 --> 00:16:22,314
[SPEAKER_00]: They often wouldn't know what kind of seeker, what kind, and then they would go, oh, who's your teacher?

215
00:16:22,354 --> 00:16:23,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what teaching do you follow?

216
00:16:23,694 --> 00:16:25,815
[SPEAKER_00]: They wouldn't have a clue, because you couldn't tell.

217
00:16:26,876 --> 00:16:29,177
[SPEAKER_00]: They're all wrapped in rags and robes and stuff like that.

218
00:16:29,217 --> 00:16:30,837
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it can be anything.

219
00:16:31,858 --> 00:16:32,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

220
00:16:37,532 --> 00:16:47,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, the thing is like this, as you say now, you're subject to other people's... Expectations, yeah, hassle, whatever else.

221
00:16:48,054 --> 00:16:54,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people stop short when there's a road, and you walk on the corner, people take their hats off, kind of thing.

222
00:16:54,716 --> 00:16:56,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep quiet, move aside, something.

223
00:16:56,716 --> 00:17:03,618
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, if you live in an environment that still includes your friends or your family or stuff like that,

224
00:17:04,701 --> 00:17:14,288
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, if you're a layperson, you have to go and hide on top of the mountain, because otherwise, you know, people would demand things from you, would need things from you, would require.

225
00:17:14,328 --> 00:17:17,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's still possible, as it was possible in the Buddha's day.

226
00:17:17,550 --> 00:17:24,515
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be technically an anāgāmī as a layperson, but it's impossible.

227
00:17:24,535 --> 00:17:26,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's unbelievably hard.

228
00:17:26,897 --> 00:17:33,061
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's hard to become an anāgāmī as a monk, let alone as a person, layperson out there in the world, today.

229
00:17:34,694 --> 00:17:40,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, it was hard back in India as well, but I guess that their lives were a bit less proliferated than ours.

230
00:17:41,238 --> 00:17:43,179
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there were less distraction, less pressure, and so on.

231
00:17:43,259 --> 00:17:45,080
[SPEAKER_00]: And the minds were also a bit less proliferative.

232
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:48,582
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh, the point is... Where is it?

233
00:17:50,003 --> 00:17:50,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Go on.

234
00:17:53,245 --> 00:17:55,146
[SPEAKER_01]: I was thinking, what's the most important thing?

235
00:17:55,666 --> 00:17:56,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Authenticity.

236
00:17:57,207 --> 00:17:57,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's the...

237
00:17:57,887 --> 00:17:58,468
[SPEAKER_01]: The number one.

238
00:17:58,888 --> 00:17:59,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

239
00:18:01,409 --> 00:18:02,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Being authentic, yeah.

240
00:18:05,350 --> 00:18:09,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But being authentic, as it's in...

241
00:18:09,252 --> 00:18:18,096
[SPEAKER_01]: I would say, you know, that's a very plain thing, actually.

242
00:18:18,116 --> 00:18:23,639
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, if you just... as I would say, authentic, you know, it is quite plain.

243
00:18:23,819 --> 00:18:24,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

244
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,842
[SPEAKER_01]: But how do you know that you aren't being authentic?

245
00:18:30,142 --> 00:18:33,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there is no... like...

246
00:18:36,622 --> 00:18:42,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Checkpoint that lights up as you pass the level, a sufficient degree of authenticity.

247
00:18:42,267 --> 00:18:49,713
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, a little beep, letting you know, okay, you're in the clear now.

248
00:18:50,013 --> 00:18:52,015
[SPEAKER_00]: You take your mask off.

249
00:18:53,276 --> 00:18:55,898
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's not like that.

250
00:18:59,841 --> 00:19:01,303
[SPEAKER_00]: You know it by...

251
00:19:02,160 --> 00:19:07,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, but various things, and I think it varies from... like, different people would recognize it when I call it different things.

252
00:19:08,382 --> 00:19:11,843
[SPEAKER_00]: But, first of all, it's very unpleasant.

253
00:19:13,024 --> 00:19:19,966
[SPEAKER_00]: So, authenticity in itself will not lead you to peace.

254
00:19:20,006 --> 00:19:28,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Becoming authentic, what it does, it removes all of the unwholesome ways that you have built up in the course of your life,

255
00:19:30,015 --> 00:19:40,481
[SPEAKER_00]: to manage or to obscure the fact that you're suffering, that existence is unpleasant, that existence is not yours, that it's dreadful, that it's terrifying.

256
00:19:41,401 --> 00:19:45,203
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you use acts of bad faith to cover that up.

257
00:19:46,204 --> 00:19:51,707
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you know, you can you can feel at ease, you can feel confident, whatever else, whatever habits you've built.

258
00:19:52,526 --> 00:19:54,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, authenticity undoes all of that.

259
00:19:55,008 --> 00:20:14,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it really exposes you back to just pure, well, of dread, anxiety, because your entire being as a whole, everything, your sense of self, you start to feel it's just completely exposed, anything, and you have no control over it, yet you don't know how to stop holding it.

260
00:20:15,963 --> 00:20:19,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's quite a dreadful situation.

261
00:20:19,625 --> 00:20:21,367
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what authenticity does.

262
00:20:22,246 --> 00:20:31,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like all of the... the existential philosophers that did talk about it, preach it, practice it themselves, could not quite sustain it.

263
00:20:32,674 --> 00:20:43,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Kierkegaard, apparently, you know, he was... apparently, on his deathbed, he said he never had a moment of peace in his life, from the moment he decided he'd become a philosopher.

264
00:20:43,883 --> 00:20:45,565
[SPEAKER_00]: But he meant that in a right sense, not kind of

265
00:20:46,512 --> 00:20:53,537
[SPEAKER_00]: theoretical, pondering, Hegelian type, but more like genuinely living what he's thinking, what he's preaching.

266
00:20:53,677 --> 00:20:58,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he was considered one of the first existentialists for those reasons.

267
00:20:58,320 --> 00:21:00,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he really made an attitude out of it.

268
00:21:00,902 --> 00:21:15,552
[SPEAKER_00]: He broke off his engagement, left the woman that he was really in love with and didn't want to accept the world position in some... because that would just made him like... well, it would kind of settle him and it would

269
00:21:16,054 --> 00:21:22,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Providing an illusion of existential safety, which he knew was not right.

270
00:21:22,737 --> 00:21:25,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Although, every ounce of his being wanted to accept it.

271
00:21:26,758 --> 00:21:30,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everybody gives in to the offers of safety.

272
00:21:30,960 --> 00:21:31,720
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it works.

273
00:21:32,481 --> 00:21:33,201
[SPEAKER_00]: But he refused.

274
00:21:33,501 --> 00:21:35,922
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, he was putridian, so he didn't know the way out.

275
00:21:38,083 --> 00:21:41,244
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but he would refuse to give in.

276
00:21:41,504 --> 00:21:45,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, basically, that for the rest of his life, he was on a... well,

277
00:21:46,766 --> 00:21:53,654
[SPEAKER_00]: On a verge of a breakdown, constantly, with, you know, facing the existential anxiety and everything else, yet not giving in.

278
00:21:54,014 --> 00:22:01,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he will be writing, like, two or three 500 pages book... 500 pages books per year, publishing.

279
00:22:01,403 --> 00:22:04,787
[SPEAKER_00]: You just look at the publishing 14, 15, 6 every year.

280
00:22:07,137 --> 00:22:09,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Massive volumes of work, because it was just so intense.

281
00:22:11,239 --> 00:22:12,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And Nietzsche as well.

282
00:22:12,160 --> 00:22:13,761
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, Nietzsche lost it in the end.

283
00:22:14,321 --> 00:22:20,745
[SPEAKER_00]: He kind of... he pushed it so far, refused to give in, and went mad as a result of it.

284
00:22:22,265 --> 00:22:32,611
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, the Buddha did say that if a person correctly, genuinely, authentically starts experiencing suffering, recognizes the nature of suffering, there are two outcomes.

285
00:22:33,371 --> 00:22:35,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Noble search or mental derangement.

286
00:22:37,570 --> 00:22:44,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Nietzsche, by refusing the Christian God, went mad, because he really left no footing for him.

287
00:22:44,815 --> 00:22:54,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Kierkegaard refused to let go of the idea of the Eternal God and Savior, which made him really, like, funny.

288
00:22:54,461 --> 00:23:01,286
[SPEAKER_00]: You read this really acute existential description, and then just switches into this God preaching weird

289
00:23:02,437 --> 00:23:04,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely discrepant stuff.

290
00:23:04,578 --> 00:23:05,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Contradictory.

291
00:23:05,898 --> 00:23:06,619
[SPEAKER_00]: So he refused.

292
00:23:06,899 --> 00:23:10,540
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what actually, I think, kept him sane.

293
00:23:10,720 --> 00:23:16,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Because without having that little bad faith, he would have fully exposed himself to the nature of suffering.

294
00:23:17,183 --> 00:23:20,404
[SPEAKER_00]: And without the Buddha's help, you cannot... you'd go mad.

295
00:23:20,884 --> 00:23:25,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you'll immediately, you know, commit to the dhamma if you've known there is such a thing.

296
00:23:29,127 --> 00:23:30,748
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what authenticity does for you.

297
00:23:33,374 --> 00:23:39,856
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's just inconceivable to succeed in any way in the dhamma without that as a starting point.

298
00:23:39,876 --> 00:23:57,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for as long as you're entertaining and holding on to the views and beliefs that you have built up as a result of covering up the nature of existence in the course of your lifetime, you cannot possibly then start applying right view and right things onto it.

299
00:23:57,364 --> 00:23:59,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're holding on to the wrong things and wrong views.

300
00:24:00,639 --> 00:24:01,480
[SPEAKER_00]: As a form of safety.

301
00:24:02,261 --> 00:24:11,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, like, you know, people can... many people, many monks can know for a fact, openly, they're not even a sotāpanna.

302
00:24:11,069 --> 00:24:12,430
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't know escape from something.

303
00:24:13,010 --> 00:24:20,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, which means they refuse to let go of things they're keeping in puthujjana, but at the same time offering them sense of safety.

304
00:24:20,838 --> 00:24:25,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, at the same time, they can believe that they are practicing the dhamma, and they're able to teach and preach about it.

305
00:24:27,113 --> 00:24:28,794
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's utter contradiction.

306
00:24:28,854 --> 00:24:31,235
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know the way yourself, you can't teach the way yourself.

307
00:24:31,916 --> 00:24:41,341
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't know the way yourself, because you're refusing to let go of the wrong ways that you have accumulated as a form of... as a form of a buffer zone, as a protection.

308
00:24:41,881 --> 00:24:52,327
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first thing you will notice, by practicing... by becoming authentic, is complete... dissolvement, so to speak, of your confidence.

309
00:24:55,412 --> 00:25:00,977
[SPEAKER_00]: The confidence that you built as a as a layperson just completely disappears in regard to everything.

310
00:25:02,858 --> 00:25:04,780
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel utterly exposed in every way.

311
00:25:06,641 --> 00:25:13,367
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, gradually, if you again don't go mad, don't do anything stupid, don't give up,

312
00:25:15,459 --> 00:25:23,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the right view can emerge from that, because you're not obstructing it by any holding that is there to delude you, to cover things up.

313
00:25:24,303 --> 00:25:25,083
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not doing that.

314
00:25:25,123 --> 00:25:27,785
[SPEAKER_00]: You're refusing to engage in an act of ignoring.

315
00:25:29,145 --> 00:25:32,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, avijjā is being... not being maintained.

316
00:25:32,227 --> 00:25:36,148
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't... you still don't know that, but that's the... that's how your experience is being set up.

317
00:25:37,429 --> 00:25:42,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, by thinking about dhamma practice and dhamma, then you actually start to recognize things that the Buddha was talking about.

318
00:25:43,267 --> 00:25:44,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is what a vijaya is.

319
00:25:46,428 --> 00:25:55,872
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a metaphysical veil of delusion, maya, or whatever else, depending on the religion.

320
00:25:55,992 --> 00:26:00,674
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not something that is, you know, to be found, to be discovered in an instant.

321
00:26:00,834 --> 00:26:03,015
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, turn the stone, it's right there.

322
00:26:03,395 --> 00:26:04,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Finally, you found it.

323
00:26:04,475 --> 00:26:04,995
[SPEAKER_00]: It was hidden.

324
00:26:05,656 --> 00:26:05,796
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

325
00:26:05,916 --> 00:26:06,776
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the very act.

326
00:26:07,976 --> 00:26:10,417
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the very attitude of your mind, day in, day out.

327
00:26:11,198 --> 00:26:11,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Bad faith.

328
00:26:14,383 --> 00:26:21,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring, actively not looking at certain things, actively not acknowledging those things.

329
00:26:21,787 --> 00:26:36,536
[SPEAKER_00]: So, technically speaking, anybody is even vaguely familiar with the suttas, has a vague idea of the dhamma, if he was to start practicing authenticity, he will naturally go in that direction.

330
00:26:37,276 --> 00:26:43,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, even that vague instruction that he came across, might be quite sufficient.

331
00:26:44,724 --> 00:26:47,085
[SPEAKER_00]: The more authentic you are, less instruction you need.

332
00:26:47,506 --> 00:27:04,795
[SPEAKER_01]: The more authentic you are, you understand whatever comes up, doesn't... if it's inauthentic, it doesn't stand up for too long.

333
00:27:05,315 --> 00:27:07,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it doesn't maintain itself.

334
00:27:08,277 --> 00:27:08,697
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

335
00:27:08,717 --> 00:27:09,458
[SPEAKER_00]: You think through.

336
00:27:09,940 --> 00:27:21,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, like, as the suttas say, that the aggregates, the five aggregates, are being taken up by your assumption, by upādāna, in the same way a fire takes up the fuel.

337
00:27:24,449 --> 00:27:28,151
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the fire burns the fuel, takes this up to be the fuel.

338
00:27:29,504 --> 00:27:31,405
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fuel maintains the fire in return.

339
00:27:31,725 --> 00:27:36,587
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the fire, through burning the fuel, spreads over that fuel and keeps burning.

340
00:27:36,627 --> 00:27:48,291
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same sense, upādāna, an assumption, through maintaining, it may... through engaging in it, it's maintaining itself in regard to your experience as a whole, which is what the five aggregates are.

341
00:27:50,392 --> 00:27:56,174
[SPEAKER_00]: So, authentic person might be a patujana, he doesn't know the dhamma, but if he recognizes

342
00:27:57,730 --> 00:28:04,972
[SPEAKER_00]: The validity, the wholesomeness of being authentic, and he starts doing it.

343
00:28:05,012 --> 00:28:15,515
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't know yet, but what he's doing, he's not actually engaging anymore in ignoring, so he's not maintaining his avijjā, he's not maintaining his upādāna, he's not maintaining his craving.

344
00:28:18,192 --> 00:28:21,614
[SPEAKER_00]: He's exposing it all and not engaging in it.

345
00:28:21,654 --> 00:28:35,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Can't a person be, like, missing or think, knowing that they are doing something which is based on lust, based on anger?

346
00:28:37,480 --> 00:28:37,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

347
00:28:38,241 --> 00:28:38,861
[SPEAKER_01]: And know it.

348
00:28:38,881 --> 00:28:41,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's craving, that's craving.

349
00:28:41,762 --> 00:28:42,903
[SPEAKER_00]: But can't you do it?

350
00:28:43,243 --> 00:28:43,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, you can't.

351
00:28:44,263 --> 00:28:46,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, well, I mean, they can, but that's not offending.

352
00:28:47,545 --> 00:28:48,025
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

353
00:28:48,658 --> 00:28:56,904
[SPEAKER_00]: They're authentic initially on the level of recognition, but they're not authentic on the level of engaging physically in those unwholesome acts, or mentally.

354
00:28:58,025 --> 00:29:03,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, like, as I said, the fact you recognize this is unwholesome, but then you choose to give in.

355
00:29:03,889 --> 00:29:06,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you abandon your authenticity right there.

356
00:29:06,751 --> 00:29:12,635
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, thinking you're authentic while you're engaging in inauthentic acts is a contradiction in terms, which means you're inauthentic.

357
00:29:14,977 --> 00:29:17,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, when you think of yourself as authentic, you're not.

358
00:29:18,693 --> 00:29:19,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you just gave in.

359
00:29:28,479 --> 00:29:42,207
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yes, as I said, a person can undo a lot of unwholesome things, which are things that maintain avidya, maintain craving, perpetuate craving and so on, even without knowing their nature.

360
00:29:43,022 --> 00:29:50,568
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when they do come across the sūtras and the dhamma, they need very little instruction, because they've already set everything up correctly.

361
00:29:50,588 --> 00:29:52,529
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like the famous story of Pākhir.

362
00:29:54,631 --> 00:30:00,095
[SPEAKER_00]: He was just doing things on his own, wearing the tree bark as his robe, and he thought he was an arahant and stuff.

363
00:30:01,256 --> 00:30:04,878
[SPEAKER_00]: And when he met the Buddha, he just gave him a line of instruction.

364
00:30:05,879 --> 00:30:09,762
[SPEAKER_00]: In sin, there will be only sin inherited, and he was an arahant right there and then.

365
00:30:10,522 --> 00:30:13,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And people are like, wow, that was a powerful instruction.

366
00:30:13,323 --> 00:30:20,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the only reason why that instruction worked is because he, although he was wearing a tree bark and doing his own thing, he was actually doing it pretty much rightly.

367
00:30:22,788 --> 00:30:30,972
[SPEAKER_00]: He was authentic to the point of he just needed the one sentence from the Buddha to just slightly tweak whatever understanding he had and that's it.

368
00:30:31,432 --> 00:30:34,634
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha meant when he said, some people have little dust in their eyes.

369
00:30:35,832 --> 00:30:41,356
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, his previous teachers, upon his arahantship, upon his enlightenment, he wanted to go and...

370
00:30:41,376 --> 00:30:42,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, who should I teach first then?

371
00:30:42,837 --> 00:30:45,178
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, I'll teach my previous teacher with very little.

372
00:30:45,218 --> 00:30:49,761
[SPEAKER_00]: He reached the base of neither perception nor perception.

373
00:30:50,822 --> 00:30:52,743
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he has very little dust in his eyes.

374
00:30:52,763 --> 00:30:56,946
[SPEAKER_00]: I just give him a sentence or two and it'll be enough for his full enlightenment.

375
00:30:58,059 --> 00:30:58,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

376
00:30:58,499 --> 00:31:06,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Because actually, reaching the state of neither perception nor perception in your contemplation, your understanding, your establishment of mind, is quite a tall order.

377
00:31:06,843 --> 00:31:15,306
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you can do that, as I said, even a sentence from the suttas will be more than sufficient for arahantship.

378
00:31:26,260 --> 00:31:48,967
[SPEAKER_00]: So if you keep that in your mind, in regard to any experience you go through, be it physical or mental, you will

379
00:31:49,947 --> 00:31:57,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind will eventually, well, start experiencing disenchantment with those things, because you're just always seeking permanence.

380
00:31:58,314 --> 00:32:06,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, without the external instruction, without the external pointer of the suttas, you can't direct that to your self-view, but you can undo pretty much everything else.

381
00:32:07,002 --> 00:32:10,124
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when it comes down to undoing your self-view, it won't be that hard.

382
00:32:13,607 --> 00:32:16,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Because self-view is maintained to all of the other wrong views.

383
00:32:17,898 --> 00:32:20,219
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's maintained through the wrong view of itself.

384
00:32:22,080 --> 00:32:27,702
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no, you can't abandon self-view and still hold other views.

385
00:32:27,722 --> 00:32:32,464
[SPEAKER_00]: So, abandoning self-view means you technically undermine all of the other wrong views.

386
00:32:32,484 --> 00:32:38,746
[SPEAKER_00]: You might still be engaging in sensuality and so on, unless you're an anāgāmī, but the nature of it has been seen.

387
00:32:38,886 --> 00:32:45,549
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just need to develop your mind to the extent necessary that that nature of sensuality doesn't pull you any longer.

388
00:32:48,069 --> 00:32:48,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Not completely, yeah.

389
00:32:48,889 --> 00:32:51,451
[SPEAKER_01]: The the 100% authentic person is is arahant.

390
00:32:51,471 --> 00:32:56,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Means there is no trace of a visual act in any shape, or form, or degree.

391
00:32:56,093 --> 00:32:57,034
[SPEAKER_01]: But exactly, yeah.

392
00:33:16,235 --> 00:33:22,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Sotāpanna, sakadāgāmī, anāgāmī, they all have corresponding degrees of bad faith.

393
00:33:22,457 --> 00:33:24,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're still not quite looking at certain things.

394
00:33:25,018 --> 00:33:27,038
[SPEAKER_00]: They lack certain things.

395
00:33:30,919 --> 00:33:37,961
[SPEAKER_01]: The ultimate of all things they do, well seems, you know, arranged,

396
00:33:47,565 --> 00:33:48,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

397
00:33:48,106 --> 00:33:56,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, knowing your motives... Well, first of all, keeping the precepts.

398
00:33:58,476 --> 00:33:59,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Eight precepts.

399
00:34:09,833 --> 00:34:13,396
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not doing anything unwholesome by body, by speech, or by mind, in that sense.

400
00:34:13,516 --> 00:34:22,164
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, especially, you start by not doing anything unwholesome by body, because, no matter how hard you think, oh yeah, I'm being authentic, you're not.

401
00:34:24,085 --> 00:34:27,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the domain of a physical action is undermining you.

402
00:34:27,488 --> 00:34:30,051
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not, you're not, you're not restraining it.

403
00:34:30,111 --> 00:34:32,413
[SPEAKER_00]: It just simply needs to be restrained, full stop.

404
00:34:33,033 --> 00:34:34,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but no, but no.

405
00:34:34,234 --> 00:34:35,275
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to be restrained.

406
00:34:35,475 --> 00:34:37,197
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, afterwards, it becomes apparent why.

407
00:34:39,577 --> 00:34:41,839
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sīla comes first.

408
00:34:42,099 --> 00:34:43,159
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not negotiable.

409
00:34:45,181 --> 00:34:46,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that would be the first step.

410
00:34:47,102 --> 00:34:59,650
[SPEAKER_00]: But once the sīla is in place, then you would start becoming more aware, more mindful of your intentions, of your motivation, why to do this, why to do that.

411
00:34:59,890 --> 00:35:03,513
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly how you distinguish whether it's wholesome or it's unwholesome.

412
00:35:05,193 --> 00:35:08,175
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were about to do something, why am I about to do it?

413
00:35:08,275 --> 00:35:09,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I want to do it?

414
00:35:09,857 --> 00:35:11,358
[SPEAKER_00]: What's my motivation behind it?

415
00:35:12,419 --> 00:35:26,010
[SPEAKER_00]: As I spoke before, sometimes what's externally... what externally appears as wholesome and great and approved by everybody else, actually can be done for unwholesome reasons.

416
00:35:27,631 --> 00:35:31,534
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that doesn't mean, like, for example, like, you know,

417
00:35:32,810 --> 00:35:33,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

418
00:35:33,130 --> 00:35:34,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Ideally, you don't want to want anything.

419
00:35:34,931 --> 00:35:35,772
[SPEAKER_00]: You just want to give.

420
00:35:35,792 --> 00:35:39,314
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you still do want, it doesn't mean you should stop making merit, because it still counts.

421
00:35:53,892 --> 00:36:08,178
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, and also, if you catch yourself wanting a better rebirth, and that's why you give merit, and then you stop giving merit altogether, well, that's like overshooting the mark as well.

422
00:36:08,518 --> 00:36:09,338
[SPEAKER_00]: That's wrong too.

423
00:36:11,679 --> 00:36:17,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you want to do is be aware of your motivation, and then change it.

424
00:36:18,847 --> 00:36:21,548
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't... that's when you stop thinking about it.

425
00:36:21,568 --> 00:36:22,568
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't stop thinking.

426
00:36:22,608 --> 00:36:28,969
[SPEAKER_00]: So you just have to fully acknowledge the fact that there is a desire for a better rebirth in the future.

427
00:36:29,909 --> 00:36:37,290
[SPEAKER_00]: And by fully acknowledging it, by being fully mindful of it, you'll end up... well, you'll stop acting out of it.

428
00:36:38,450 --> 00:36:47,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Because only when you're not mindful of things that are in the background, as like a more general intention that's motivating you, then you're being controlled by it.

429
00:36:47,993 --> 00:37:00,680
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, when people sometimes, they kind of know why they're doing something, but they refuse to acknowledge to themselves, and then somebody points it out, and he kind of stops them in their shoes, like, oh, yeah, it's obvious.

430
00:37:01,861 --> 00:37:05,322
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, that's an act of bad faith, and that's what you don't want to entertain.

431
00:37:05,402 --> 00:37:13,387
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you always keep reviewing your mind as to why am I doing this, why am I doing that, why am I saying this, why do I want to do this, why am I about to say that?

432
00:37:15,080 --> 00:37:18,261
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you see something unwholesome, it doesn't mean, okay, I'll stop doing everything now.

433
00:37:18,281 --> 00:37:19,602
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because that's another act.

434
00:37:19,962 --> 00:37:22,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, now, you're doing out of fear of unwholesome.

435
00:37:23,103 --> 00:37:25,384
[SPEAKER_00]: But acting out of fear is also unwholesome.

436
00:37:27,165 --> 00:37:33,128
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you end up revolving around, you know, panic or whatever else, confusion, doubt, which is all unwholesome.

437
00:37:34,348 --> 00:37:37,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you actually, no, you stop and review it.

438
00:37:37,410 --> 00:37:40,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I want to make merit because I want to be reborn in heaven.

439
00:37:42,826 --> 00:37:43,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Making merit counts.

440
00:37:44,588 --> 00:37:46,590
[SPEAKER_00]: You just reflect upon the entire situation.

441
00:37:46,690 --> 00:37:49,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Making merit counts, no matter what I want or not.

442
00:37:49,973 --> 00:37:55,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, that merit will be greater if I don't act out of two selfish reasons.

443
00:37:58,220 --> 00:38:06,288
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, you still value, basically, you still recognize that the act of making merit is valid, whether you're selfish or not.

444
00:38:07,068 --> 00:38:08,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So you don't deny it entirely.

445
00:38:09,465 --> 00:38:17,168
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, what you try to do is, while making merit or whatever else, just focus on different aspects, because there are other intentions present.

446
00:38:18,969 --> 00:38:26,132
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, like, yeah, you want... the primary intention is you want to be reborn in heaven, but also you want to help to whatever you're given.

447
00:38:27,813 --> 00:38:37,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Or simply, you know that, well, okay, I can't stop wishing to be reborn in heaven, but what I can do is reflect on the nature of the heaven.

448
00:38:38,742 --> 00:38:42,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And also see that that's something impermanent that will not last forever.

449
00:38:42,865 --> 00:38:49,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you experience disenchantment in regard to that which you cannot stop wishing for.

450
00:38:50,791 --> 00:38:55,875
[SPEAKER_00]: And automatically, exactly, the pull of your desire towards it stops.

451
00:38:55,915 --> 00:39:02,880
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still a fact that if you give, you will be reborn in heaven eventually as a result of the greatness of your gifts.

452
00:39:03,580 --> 00:39:05,762
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it was nothing to do with you from the beginning.

453
00:39:06,813 --> 00:39:10,155
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just aspects you were focusing on and you're craving taking over.

454
00:39:12,556 --> 00:39:16,639
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you reflect that even that is impermanent, you'll stop craving it.

455
00:39:17,259 --> 00:39:18,180
[SPEAKER_00]: You might still want it.

456
00:39:18,460 --> 00:39:20,601
[SPEAKER_00]: In a sense, it's factual.

457
00:39:20,701 --> 00:39:22,502
[SPEAKER_00]: It will happen as one of the outcomes.

458
00:39:23,683 --> 00:39:24,664
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's absolutely fine.

459
00:39:26,445 --> 00:39:31,928
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the problem that people, when they kind of experience... well, it feels like action paralysis.

460
00:39:34,029 --> 00:39:46,798
[SPEAKER_00]: When you get caught up, like, oh, you see you're about to do something unwholesome, or something that comes from the unwholesome reason, but it's not necessarily unwholesome, but then you just stop doing everything, you just stop, shut it down, shut... no, nothing, nothing.

461
00:39:46,879 --> 00:39:48,159
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm saying no to everything.

462
00:39:48,460 --> 00:39:53,143
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't possibly sustain it, because, as I said, that's an act that comes out of that unwholesome.

463
00:39:54,884 --> 00:40:02,290
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you actually have to be able to allow it to arise, and then recognize where it comes from, and affect that, not just

464
00:40:02,865 --> 00:40:05,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Throw the baby with the water and everything else.

465
00:40:11,013 --> 00:40:13,175
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you uproot the desire as well.

466
00:40:13,495 --> 00:40:14,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Any desire for that matter.

467
00:40:16,938 --> 00:40:25,627
[SPEAKER_00]: When you desire something, well, then you reflect on what you're desiring, on the final goal, final outcome of that desire.

468
00:40:26,727 --> 00:40:36,450
[SPEAKER_00]: You reflect on that long enough, as impermanent, as something that inherently cannot provide satisfaction, and automatically, you... your desire fades.

469
00:40:37,311 --> 00:40:52,596
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you directly start to try to tackle your desire, I'm not wishing it, you just end up winding yourself up, more and more, like a dog running, tied to a post, running around it, and just making the... making the leash shorter and shorter and shorter.

470
00:40:53,877 --> 00:40:54,737
[SPEAKER_01]: A suit of...

471
00:40:56,527 --> 00:40:57,227
[SPEAKER_01]: One's that are dear.

472
00:40:57,988 --> 00:40:58,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

473
00:40:58,568 --> 00:41:00,889
[SPEAKER_01]: One's that are dear, you know, clean, the same being.

474
00:41:01,250 --> 00:41:02,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, the dear ones, okay.

475
00:41:02,270 --> 00:41:02,931
[SPEAKER_01]: The dear ones, yeah.

476
00:41:05,012 --> 00:41:05,552
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

477
00:41:09,594 --> 00:41:10,575
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about a non-same.

478
00:41:13,296 --> 00:41:20,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Having family and so on helps you to see that they are impermanent.

479
00:41:22,350 --> 00:41:43,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And what I just said in terms of the desire, and what you're just saying in terms of the impermanent, yeah, that's basically... that's the difference between thinking the world is the problem and recognizing that actually the problem is your attitude of craving.

480
00:41:44,689 --> 00:41:49,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Because by default, you don't see your craving, you immediately project, you think the world is a problem.

481
00:41:50,151 --> 00:41:58,495
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ah, the dear ones are cause for suffering, my family is cause for my pain, friends are cause for suffering, attachment is a cause of pain.

482
00:41:58,735 --> 00:42:00,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Deny, deny, deny.

483
00:42:00,736 --> 00:42:07,279
[SPEAKER_00]: I will run away, I will abandon everybody, I will not talk to anybody ever again, because attachment is the suffering.

484
00:42:07,799 --> 00:42:13,242
[SPEAKER_00]: But you project it onto the world, and you think that by running away, you will abandon the attachment, but you won't.

485
00:42:13,682 --> 00:42:15,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Because attachment is actually your attitude.

486
00:42:16,063 --> 00:42:23,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you stay in the world, or relatively stay in the world, you will not run away or whatever else, or project out there.

487
00:42:23,787 --> 00:42:33,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, exactly, by reflecting upon the impermanence, upon the like inherent dissolution of it, eventually, you will experience disenchantment.

488
00:42:33,933 --> 00:42:37,674
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the abandoning of attachment, not running away.

489
00:42:38,415 --> 00:42:41,997
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, you know, you can have arahants living in the middle of a village.

490
00:42:42,992 --> 00:42:43,752
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha did.

491
00:42:43,772 --> 00:42:46,873
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, well, let the monks who want to live in caves, live in caves.

492
00:42:46,913 --> 00:42:53,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Let those who want to live in the cities, live in the cities, for as long as it brings them arahant.

493
00:42:53,796 --> 00:43:05,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the famous sutra that I often actually quote, the bait, the simile of the bait, when people unbearably eat the bait, eat the food, and Mara lays out for them the deer herd, and then they...

494
00:43:06,275 --> 00:43:07,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Mahārāja does whatever he wants.

495
00:43:07,755 --> 00:43:15,697
[SPEAKER_00]: Then other people see that, other herd sees that, and then they shun the bait completely, run off in the mountains, say no to everything, but they can't sustain that.

496
00:43:16,557 --> 00:43:22,619
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then they come back, and then they have no choice but to eat unworthily, and then again Mahārāja takes control over them.

497
00:43:22,659 --> 00:43:30,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the third deer herd is the one that sees this bird and says, well, what if we stay in the domain of the bait, but eat it carefully?

498
00:43:32,261 --> 00:43:33,341
[SPEAKER_00]: And then Mahārāja can't see them.

499
00:43:33,955 --> 00:43:38,661
[SPEAKER_00]: They're right there, but they're not catching on to the bait, because he can see only those who grab the bait.

500
00:43:41,004 --> 00:43:44,228
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're right in the mara's domain, but he can't see you.

501
00:43:44,488 --> 00:43:49,995
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's often in the suttas, and the Buddha says, the evil one, the unworthy one, can't see me.

502
00:43:51,017 --> 00:43:52,018
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm invisible to them.

503
00:43:59,071 --> 00:44:01,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Next, to see that...

504
00:44:01,632 --> 00:44:02,573
[SPEAKER_01]: I really like the verse.

505
00:44:02,593 --> 00:44:03,233
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the...

506
00:44:04,254 --> 00:44:06,194
[SPEAKER_01]: He's telling this woman to reflect.

507
00:44:06,214 --> 00:44:08,215
[SPEAKER_01]: What he's telling this Kuni to reflect.

508
00:44:08,556 --> 00:44:09,276
[SPEAKER_01]: She lost her son.

509
00:44:09,996 --> 00:44:10,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh-huh.

510
00:44:12,377 --> 00:44:14,659
[SPEAKER_01]: He came without your permission.

511
00:44:15,999 --> 00:44:17,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I thought that was fair.

512
00:44:17,700 --> 00:44:18,740
[SPEAKER_01]: What is there to do for...

513
00:44:20,201 --> 00:44:21,422
[SPEAKER_01]: He came from somewhere.

514
00:44:21,742 --> 00:44:22,842
[SPEAKER_01]: He was leaving another way.

515
00:44:23,163 --> 00:44:24,083
[SPEAKER_01]: This way or that way.

516
00:44:24,163 --> 00:44:26,044
[SPEAKER_01]: But it went without permission.

517
00:44:27,605 --> 00:44:28,025
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's...

518
00:44:30,489 --> 00:44:32,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it becomes utterly pointless.

519
00:44:32,890 --> 00:44:38,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, you can keep the civil

520
00:44:58,785 --> 00:45:07,350
[SPEAKER_00]: The relationship normalizes itself, but internally... And again, it's different than just saying, no, I'm refusing to have contact with them, and things like that.

521
00:45:08,371 --> 00:45:15,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there would be... Well, that means you're still attached to it, now you're just denying the whole thing, because you can't free yourself from it.

522
00:45:16,056 --> 00:45:26,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like that... unaware group of... that they had... that saw people being controlled on account of the bait, then they shunned the bait, and they ran away from it.

523
00:45:26,382 --> 00:45:28,083
[SPEAKER_00]: But then they came back, because they had no choice.

524
00:45:30,733 --> 00:45:31,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the thing.

525
00:45:31,453 --> 00:45:36,856
[SPEAKER_00]: If you internally experience disenchantment in regard to it, externally, you could be very much in the middle of it.

526
00:45:38,477 --> 00:45:40,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, there are things that externally you cannot do.

527
00:45:41,578 --> 00:45:47,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you cannot break the eight precepts, so to speak, intentionally, and around.

528
00:45:48,141 --> 00:45:56,245
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot intentionally kill, intentionally engage in sexual intercourse, intentionally steal or lie or things like that.

529
00:45:56,265 --> 00:45:57,005
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't do that.

530
00:45:58,343 --> 00:46:03,146
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the certain awholesome things and the external manifestations of them are entirely gone.

531
00:46:04,487 --> 00:46:13,832
[SPEAKER_00]: But everything else, like... which is why often arahants are not being recognized externally by others, including other arahants sometimes.

532
00:46:20,756 --> 00:46:23,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, see, this is the toughest one.

533
00:46:27,936 --> 00:46:32,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's fundamentally speaking, but whether he wants it or not, makes no difference.

534
00:46:32,540 --> 00:46:36,603
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you experience this enchantment,

535
00:46:50,906 --> 00:46:57,588
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like, you won't have a problem talking about it, because it's not... it ceases to be like talking about myself.

536
00:46:58,469 --> 00:47:02,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Externally, again, people can think, oh, he's full of himself.

537
00:47:02,590 --> 00:47:11,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the first thing the Buddha said upon his enlightenment, when he was walking on the road, when that man met him and says, oh, your senses are clear and bright, you look

538
00:47:12,672 --> 00:47:17,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Peaceful, uh, whose teaching did you follow to reach that peace and clarity?

539
00:47:17,415 --> 00:47:19,497
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, I don't follow anyone, Steve.

540
00:47:19,577 --> 00:47:20,357
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm my own leader.

541
00:47:20,377 --> 00:47:21,218
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm my own teacher.

542
00:47:21,278 --> 00:47:22,419
[SPEAKER_00]: I've enlightened myself.

543
00:47:22,459 --> 00:47:23,679
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm the greatest in the world.

544
00:47:24,260 --> 00:47:27,982
[SPEAKER_00]: And this man says, right, so be it, friend, and walks off.

545
00:47:28,663 --> 00:47:31,024
[SPEAKER_00]: He thought, like, good God, he's just full of himself.

546
00:47:31,244 --> 00:47:34,327
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not gonna talk to him, and left him.

547
00:47:35,667 --> 00:47:37,108
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha was not wrong in saying any of it.

548
00:47:37,529 --> 00:47:38,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he's the greatest in the world.

549
00:47:40,026 --> 00:47:47,568
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, if you say that to putujana, to somebody who is not free from the self-view, they cannot possibly not take it in a conceited sense.

550
00:47:49,168 --> 00:48:01,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why, you know, when Sister Vajira, in the letters, only later realized the true meaning of anathimāni, non-conceit, because she thought Jñānavīra was full of himself, and she really had a go at him, trying to really challenge him.

551
00:48:01,831 --> 00:48:08,073
[SPEAKER_00]: But when she failed, and then come to see the reason, and get right with him, she realized, no, actually, he was truly humble.

552
00:48:10,168 --> 00:48:18,675
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, you read that externally, you couldn't tell, if you wouldn't know, if you wouldn't yourself have a reference point for what the real humility means.

553
00:48:21,277 --> 00:48:36,150
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the other Sutta, when the Buddha... when that woman was... we spoke about it the other day, when she was saying to Nanda, oh, how come my uncle and my father, although they were the same, they were reborn in different heavens, and so on, and the Buddha said, because of different faculties, and then he goes on saying,

554
00:48:37,660 --> 00:48:49,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, a person can externally be pleasing to everybody, to monks, to lay people, just the greatest person, the humblest, the meekest and everything, but he holds on to the wrong views.

555
00:48:51,809 --> 00:49:01,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, basically, externally, whatever he is, it's utterly irrelevant, because the wrong views will take him down, not bring him to the better rebirth in the future.

556
00:49:02,892 --> 00:49:19,804
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, constantly, like, opposite of that, you're a person who is prone to anger, prone to being irritated, prone to lust, prone to aversion, externally, you know, people think he's a bit of a, you know, a prick, and... but he has right views.

557
00:49:20,724 --> 00:49:22,946
[SPEAKER_00]: He did develop right views.

558
00:49:22,966 --> 00:49:25,508
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, he will go up afterwards.

559
00:49:26,257 --> 00:49:28,538
[SPEAKER_00]: He's far from ideal, but he's still...

560
00:49:28,778 --> 00:49:30,638
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the external is not the measure.

561
00:49:31,179 --> 00:49:34,060
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be an indicator, but it's far from being a measure.

562
00:49:42,582 --> 00:49:47,604
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, fewness of the wishes and so on, that's an internal attitude.

563
00:49:48,864 --> 00:49:53,806
[SPEAKER_00]: But, depending on the person's faculties, are bringing... and, you know,

564
00:49:54,469 --> 00:50:06,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Depending on previous lives and habits that have been cultivated, like neutral habits that have been cultivated for so long, they might be speaking like a really, really arrogant whatever.

565
00:50:06,853 --> 00:50:07,493
[SPEAKER_00]: I know it all.

566
00:50:07,553 --> 00:50:08,453
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm full of myself.

567
00:50:08,753 --> 00:50:12,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Although, they might be an arahant, or on the way to become one.

568
00:50:15,835 --> 00:50:18,136
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where Putujan's authenticity comes in.

569
00:50:19,397 --> 00:50:21,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you shouldn't...

570
00:50:21,217 --> 00:50:24,158
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, it's how you should... you should not... you should not...

571
00:50:25,303 --> 00:50:26,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Criticize anything.

572
00:50:26,796 --> 00:50:27,784
[SPEAKER_00]: You should accept everything.

573
00:50:29,113 --> 00:50:31,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, every view you should accept.

574
00:50:31,635 --> 00:50:32,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Every view is the right view.

575
00:50:33,536 --> 00:50:34,637
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's nonsense.

576
00:50:34,757 --> 00:50:35,718
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't work like that.

577
00:50:36,138 --> 00:50:38,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you do need to distinguish between right and wrong.

578
00:50:39,140 --> 00:50:45,865
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're authentic, you'll know, okay, this makes more sense, or this doesn't make so much sense.

579
00:50:46,846 --> 00:50:52,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't fully dismiss, nor do you fully commit, in a sense, you don't forget that you're still a putrugena.

580
00:50:53,631 --> 00:50:55,813
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you follow the one that makes more sense.

581
00:50:56,525 --> 00:50:59,126
[SPEAKER_00]: And you keep reflecting upon it and re-evaluating it.

582
00:50:59,946 --> 00:51:08,690
[SPEAKER_00]: But because you clearly keep in your mind that you're still a patujana, you never fully shut the door to things being different.

583
00:51:09,430 --> 00:51:20,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if sometime down the line, you now find out that this view was utterly wrong, you won't have a problem dropping it in an instant and finding a better one, the one that makes more sense.

584
00:51:21,515 --> 00:51:24,696
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're inauthentic though, you will have a big problem out of it.

585
00:51:25,915 --> 00:51:36,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why, you know, you get 40-50 Vāsāyājans, and if their view gets challenged, it's just the entire existence is being kind of trembling.

586
00:51:37,477 --> 00:51:37,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

587
00:51:37,657 --> 00:51:44,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they shut the doors very early, and went down the wrong lane, too far to be able to come back.

588
00:51:45,278 --> 00:51:46,378
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not optional anymore.

589
00:51:46,558 --> 00:51:49,819
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you don't want to happen to you, or to anybody else.

590
00:51:50,439 --> 00:51:53,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to be authentic, and then, exactly that.

591
00:51:53,760 --> 00:51:55,360
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you think you're right, well,

592
00:51:56,138 --> 00:51:56,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Test it.

593
00:51:57,778 --> 00:52:02,219
[SPEAKER_00]: As I told you before... Oh, wow!

594
00:52:02,319 --> 00:52:18,382
[SPEAKER_00]: As I've told you before, like... when I started reading philosophy and dhamma and the suttas, the... being in nothingness made quite a big impact on me.

595
00:52:18,522 --> 00:52:21,903
[SPEAKER_00]: It was quite insightful and...

596
00:52:24,235 --> 00:52:25,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like a revelation.

597
00:52:25,375 --> 00:52:27,056
[SPEAKER_00]: I read it, I think, six or seven times.

598
00:52:28,476 --> 00:52:30,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I found Yanavera, started reading Yanavera.

599
00:52:30,937 --> 00:52:36,119
[SPEAKER_00]: That made even more sense, because it was like existential philosophy attitude about the suttas.

600
00:52:36,179 --> 00:52:38,580
[SPEAKER_00]: It was exactly like, oh, the missing link, and so on.

601
00:52:38,620 --> 00:52:39,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I really got into that.

602
00:52:40,661 --> 00:52:46,963
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, found Yanavera was a lot influenced by Sartre, and his writing, and even his way of writing was similar.

603
00:52:48,163 --> 00:52:50,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is great for me, because I was already very familiar with it.

604
00:52:50,765 --> 00:52:57,250
[SPEAKER_00]: But then he kept saying, well, it's like, in reference to this or that, how sātra is wrong here, sātra is wrong there.

605
00:52:57,650 --> 00:52:58,570
[SPEAKER_00]: And I couldn't see it.

606
00:52:59,671 --> 00:53:01,172
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't see how sātra was wrong.

607
00:53:01,212 --> 00:53:05,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for me, like, I could have deluded myself and said, well, it doesn't matter.

608
00:53:06,016 --> 00:53:08,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I saw what sātra meant, and I thought I was right.

609
00:53:09,178 --> 00:53:11,820
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, Nyanavidā says what sātra meant here is wrong.

610
00:53:13,091 --> 00:53:15,013
[SPEAKER_00]: And I could have deluded myself and said, well, Nyanavira is wrong.

611
00:53:15,113 --> 00:53:16,114
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't know what he's talking about.

612
00:53:16,134 --> 00:53:16,975
[SPEAKER_00]: He's following himself.

613
00:53:17,415 --> 00:53:19,257
[SPEAKER_00]: If I was too afraid to abandon my views.

614
00:53:19,277 --> 00:53:19,818
[SPEAKER_00]: But I wasn't.

615
00:53:20,799 --> 00:53:22,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So then I kept reading Nyanavira.

616
00:53:22,120 --> 00:53:32,211
[SPEAKER_00]: But then I was becoming afraid to read Being and Nothingness, or to find any reference to Sātre, because in my mind, I felt like I'm gaining understanding of what Nyanavira is saying.

617
00:53:33,152 --> 00:53:36,014
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if I were to go back and read Sātre's work,

618
00:53:36,922 --> 00:53:45,669
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I would still feel him right, there would be a contradiction in terms, because I can't have both, Yanavera right and Sārcha right, because Yanavera said Sārcha is wrong.

619
00:53:47,190 --> 00:53:54,516
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I fully understand both, I'll see how Yanavera is right, and I'll see how Sārcha was wrong.

620
00:53:56,278 --> 00:53:58,079
[SPEAKER_00]: And lots of people are actually afraid to do that.

621
00:53:58,860 --> 00:54:05,586
[SPEAKER_00]: They're afraid to undermine their own views, which means they're afraid to upgrade them, because upgrade is a form of a change.

622
00:54:06,298 --> 00:54:07,219
[SPEAKER_00]: And they're afraid to do that.

623
00:54:08,199 --> 00:54:14,084
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for a while, I was afraid, but I kept going with Nyanavira, but I couldn't, you know, I wouldn't open anything of Sārcā.

624
00:54:14,104 --> 00:54:20,509
[SPEAKER_00]: I had the whole 12-stack collection my mother brought me from Serbia, that I bought ages ago, all of his works.

625
00:54:21,230 --> 00:54:26,313
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, I remember, once I was like, well, you know, it's not postponing, I really have to see where I'm at.

626
00:54:27,384 --> 00:54:36,230
[SPEAKER_00]: And I opened the Transcendence of Ego, his kind of shorter work, which I remember it was so... quite a revelation for me to read the whole thing.

627
00:54:36,250 --> 00:54:38,171
[SPEAKER_00]: It was like so, like, wow, accurate, whatever.

628
00:54:38,912 --> 00:54:42,054
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was afraid I won't see his wrong views there.

629
00:54:42,674 --> 00:54:46,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Fortunately enough, like, first, second paragraph, it became, oh,

630
00:54:47,439 --> 00:54:48,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, yeah.

631
00:54:48,599 --> 00:54:55,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, yeah, I did actually understand in the Veda, at that point, sufficiently enough to see how such was wrong.

632
00:54:55,782 --> 00:54:57,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Which was clearly a measure of progress for me.

633
00:54:58,823 --> 00:54:59,844
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then I just kept going.

634
00:55:01,304 --> 00:55:06,026
[SPEAKER_00]: And that also means that you cease to be afraid from any challenge of any other view.

635
00:55:06,486 --> 00:55:11,928
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then I will go to library and pick up... Well, I would pick up knowingly stuff that are wrong views.

636
00:55:12,528 --> 00:55:16,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Like all the nonsense they have in the West, in the libraries, all the new age

637
00:55:17,710 --> 00:55:21,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Books and then, you know, the acid head gurus from India or whatever else.

638
00:55:22,593 --> 00:55:24,814
[SPEAKER_00]: I wouldn't bother with that because it's just too plainly wrong.

639
00:55:25,154 --> 00:55:35,560
[SPEAKER_00]: But then the stuff that was kind of borderline, I would get that and read it and try to see it's not just, oh, this is wrong because I don't like him and I know what he got up to or whatever else.

640
00:55:35,620 --> 00:55:35,800
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

641
00:55:36,460 --> 00:55:41,743
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to read the views, I had to understand the views, and I had to see how and where they were wrong.

642
00:55:43,174 --> 00:56:01,877
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had a similar thing when I was reading Samana Pāla Sutta, the fruits of the ascetic life in Dīgha Nikaya, when the Buddha lists the six main kind of bases for the wrong view.

643
00:56:01,917 --> 00:56:11,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when people think there's nobody killed, and you can go and just chop everybody's head off along the east side of Ganges, and this side of Ganges, and nobody ever dies, it's just elements.

644
00:56:12,202 --> 00:56:15,525
[SPEAKER_00]: First time I read that, to me, felt every of those views felt right.

645
00:56:16,126 --> 00:56:17,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, there is no self.

646
00:56:17,847 --> 00:56:19,109
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no good in reality.

647
00:56:19,149 --> 00:56:19,449
[SPEAKER_00]: There is...

648
00:56:19,749 --> 00:56:21,871
[SPEAKER_00]: I was a layman back then, 18, 19 years old.

649
00:56:22,452 --> 00:56:23,072
[SPEAKER_01]: By chance.

650
00:56:23,353 --> 00:56:23,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

651
00:56:23,833 --> 00:56:27,537
[SPEAKER_00]: But then as I'm reading, I thought the Buddha is just listing these great views.

652
00:56:28,017 --> 00:56:29,879
[SPEAKER_00]: But then he says, all these views are wrong.

653
00:56:30,019 --> 00:56:31,180
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was, oh my goodness.

654
00:56:31,480 --> 00:56:34,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I just thought they're all right.

655
00:56:35,044 --> 00:56:36,285
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't see how they're wrong.

656
00:56:37,495 --> 00:56:43,639
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, I could have just done a blind eye to it, or I could have made the effort and see exactly why they're wrong, which is what I've done.

657
00:56:44,639 --> 00:56:49,642
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what he... like, when he talks, you should interrogate, investigate, re-evaluate.

658
00:56:49,662 --> 00:56:50,843
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the practice is.

659
00:56:51,690 --> 00:56:59,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, you know, sitting and staring at the floor or nodding your head down and watching your breath until you fall asleep.

660
00:56:59,152 --> 00:56:59,333
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

661
00:56:59,533 --> 00:57:00,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Be like full-on active.

662
00:57:01,093 --> 00:57:11,036
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you're into samādhi, if you're a samādhi type, when people often used to disregard, not making, like, to justify not making the effort to understand the suttas.

663
00:57:11,136 --> 00:57:12,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm a samādhi type.

664
00:57:12,737 --> 00:57:16,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, only if you're an arahant, you can say whether you're a samādhi type or vipassana type.

665
00:57:16,818 --> 00:57:17,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Not before that.

666
00:57:20,198 --> 00:57:24,481
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you go to sit and meditate, you should be developing the right views.

667
00:57:25,781 --> 00:57:27,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like not doing anything.

668
00:57:28,783 --> 00:57:29,824
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the practice is.

669
00:57:30,284 --> 00:57:35,808
[SPEAKER_00]: That constant drilling, uprooting, abandoning, upgrading, whatever else, whatever it takes,

670
00:57:36,600 --> 00:57:58,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Until you... until your very knowledge, not a chance, not a circumstance, nothing given externally to you that can be taken, until something that you fully developed and you fully understood by yourself is the result, is the cause of you being free from suffering and free from possibility to suffer ever again in the future.

671
00:57:58,564 --> 00:58:00,405
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the practice is and that's where it should end.

672
00:58:00,425 --> 00:58:01,225
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where it should lead.

673
00:58:05,180 --> 00:58:05,921
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you need to have.

674
00:58:06,181 --> 00:58:07,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Read the suttas.

675
00:58:08,923 --> 00:58:18,771
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Nibiru is such an important book, because it was that... it was that, like, necessary starting point, just telling you how to start.

676
00:58:19,271 --> 00:58:30,019
[SPEAKER_00]: I often say to people, well, even if you don't get anything more from clearing the path, at least if you get the attitude that you should have towards the suttas, that's a good starting point.

677
00:58:30,039 --> 00:58:33,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I said, like, yeah, you can help people,

678
00:58:33,800 --> 00:58:41,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely disregarding certain parts of the suttas, they're contradicting their views, yet at the same time think they're practicing the suttas.

679
00:58:41,503 --> 00:58:42,663
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not practicing the dhamma.

680
00:58:42,783 --> 00:58:43,644
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't work like that.

681
00:58:44,464 --> 00:58:47,705
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to understand something from the suttas, you'd be at least a sotāpanna.

682
00:58:47,925 --> 00:58:51,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, if you're not a sotāpanna, there is no understanding.

683
00:58:51,647 --> 00:58:52,927
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no grey area there.

684
00:58:57,873 --> 00:59:03,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was... like, it's just such an obvious thing, but even I needed to hear it from the Nabiru.

685
00:59:03,275 --> 00:59:04,536
[SPEAKER_00]: It just doesn't occur to you.

686
00:59:05,116 --> 00:59:09,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially because all the contemporary stuff that's out there, that it's telling you otherwise.

687
00:59:10,218 --> 00:59:12,339
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm not reading, because the Nabiru keeps saying that.

688
00:59:13,900 --> 00:59:22,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when we saw the Qur'an, I was like... and I think... and then you look at the suttas and you realize, yeah, it says that, but you just never pay attention to it.

689
00:59:27,038 --> 00:59:27,919
[SPEAKER_00]: On many occasions.

690
00:59:38,587 --> 00:59:39,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's continue.

691
00:59:39,568 --> 00:59:43,371
[SPEAKER_01]: If you cross over things, you don't pay attention to them.

692
00:59:43,391 --> 00:59:43,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

693
00:59:43,531 --> 00:59:44,712
[SPEAKER_00]: They stand out more than I do.

694
00:59:44,732 --> 00:59:49,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, let's... let's be... it became apparent to me in early days, eh...

695
00:59:49,455 --> 00:59:52,778
[SPEAKER_00]: I was doing lots of walking meditation and contemplating things, and I realized, well, hold on.

696
00:59:53,599 --> 00:59:55,060
[SPEAKER_00]: I am contemplating the dhamma.

697
00:59:55,911 --> 01:00:05,765
[SPEAKER_00]: But what makes me think that the tools that are used for contemplation, basic concepts that are used for contemplation, what makes me think that that's right?

698
01:00:06,886 --> 01:00:07,627
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I know that?

699
01:00:08,508 --> 01:00:12,814
[SPEAKER_00]: You just assume that the tools that were given to you were right, and then you just start working with them.

700
01:00:13,849 --> 01:00:14,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Hold on.

701
01:00:14,810 --> 01:00:15,690
[SPEAKER_00]: What if they're not right?

702
01:00:16,131 --> 01:00:19,673
[SPEAKER_00]: It means the outcome that you make with those tools will never be right either.

703
01:00:20,434 --> 01:00:26,158
[SPEAKER_00]: And the tools I'm referring to are these basic concepts such as consciousness, perception, feeling, intentionality.

704
01:00:26,979 --> 01:00:28,840
[SPEAKER_00]: And it became apparent to me that...

705
01:00:29,521 --> 01:00:30,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Your definitions.

706
01:00:30,622 --> 01:00:31,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

707
01:00:31,842 --> 01:00:41,950
[SPEAKER_00]: What I, in twenty-something years, what I have developed, what I've kind of gathered,

708
01:00:42,506 --> 01:00:52,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness, to me, well, I'm a puthujjana, which means my understanding of what consciousness is, is puthujjana, which means there is no understanding.

709
01:00:53,812 --> 01:01:03,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was in the early days, and I realized that, okay, so actually it's not about finding, it's just undoing stuff that you already have, because what you have, you got it wrongly.

710
01:01:05,155 --> 01:01:07,816
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then I switched and started doing my walking meditation.

711
01:01:07,856 --> 01:01:09,377
[SPEAKER_00]: I was in Harlem, in a little room.

712
01:01:09,397 --> 01:01:11,177
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it was just maybe four or five steps.

713
01:01:12,058 --> 01:01:12,838
[SPEAKER_00]: One way, the other way.

714
01:01:12,938 --> 01:01:14,378
[SPEAKER_00]: One way, diagonally.

715
01:01:14,859 --> 01:01:15,779
[SPEAKER_00]: From wall to wall.

716
01:01:15,799 --> 01:01:17,640
[SPEAKER_00]: But it was enough to keep me going.

717
01:01:18,200 --> 01:01:19,881
[SPEAKER_00]: And I kept doing it there.

718
01:01:20,041 --> 01:01:20,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, I just...

719
01:01:20,641 --> 01:01:22,001
[SPEAKER_00]: I would literally just take up...

720
01:01:22,061 --> 01:01:22,782
[SPEAKER_00]: I was still on the Garika.

721
01:01:23,842 --> 01:01:25,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Take up consciousness.

722
01:01:26,163 --> 01:01:28,384
[SPEAKER_00]: And just keep thinking what consciousness is.

723
01:01:28,524 --> 01:01:31,565
[SPEAKER_00]: And I don't mean referring to, you know, some...

724
01:01:33,168 --> 01:01:43,998
[SPEAKER_00]: ...psychological descriptions, nonsense, whatever else, but just generally, okay, if I say consciousness out loud, right now, what does that stand for, right now, in my experience?

725
01:01:44,058 --> 01:01:45,779
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to stand for something.

726
01:01:46,700 --> 01:01:48,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you couldn't be able to even say it.

727
01:01:50,763 --> 01:01:57,329
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can do that in regard to everything, but you want to bring it down to the most general, most fundamental terms.

728
01:01:57,349 --> 01:01:58,190
[SPEAKER_00]: The five aggregates.

729
01:01:59,577 --> 01:02:03,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Matter, feeling, perception, intentionality, and consciousness.

730
01:02:05,760 --> 01:02:19,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you start thinking like that, and you realize it's not about building things, just... not even bothering with the tools, just undoing that which I thought are tools, because all of that was taken for granted, and that was already the problem.

731
01:02:20,328 --> 01:02:21,189
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you undo that.

732
01:02:21,269 --> 01:02:22,149
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you have to do.

733
01:02:22,810 --> 01:02:26,232
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that you have to undo the wrong tools and then build the right thing.

734
01:02:26,272 --> 01:02:26,412
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

735
01:02:26,572 --> 01:02:28,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Undoing that means building the right thing.

